WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:04.360
The first step is to have a Twinmotion project ready.

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.800
Use the TM2UE Twinmotion file that's been provided to you.

3
00:00:09.769 --> 00:00:12.970
You also need an Unreal Engine project on the receiving end.

4
00:00:13.539 --> 00:00:18.199
But before you create one, you will need to install a dedicated Twinmotion plugin.

5
00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:22.469
Use the Epic Games Launcher to access Marketplace.

6
00:00:24.629 --> 00:00:29.730
Look up the plugin named Datasmith Twinmotion Content for Unreal Engine.

7
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:36.950
Install it to Unreal Engine. In my case, I've already installed it to the 5.3 version.

8
00:00:40.649 --> 00:00:53.500
Next, start UE5 and create a new project using the blank architectural template and name it TM2UE5.

9
00:00:55.799 --> 00:00:58.799
First order of business is to ensure the plugin is enabled.

10
00:00:59.299 --> 00:01:02.799
Go to the plugin's dialog and search for Twinmotion.

11
00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:11.719
Enable the Datasmith Twinmotion Content plugin you just installed and restart the project.

12
00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:17.549
Once that's done, close the plugin's dialog and create a new default level.

13
00:01:25.599 --> 00:01:29.500
Set the Sun and Sky to a more reasonable intensity of 100 lux.

14
00:01:33.819 --> 00:01:38.120
Adjust the post-process volume's exposure to a more constant setting.

15
00:01:49.849 --> 00:01:53.750
Delete the camera and the floor. You won't need them for this project.

16
00:01:55.140 --> 00:02:00.739
Finally, move all remaining actors into a Lighting folder for better scene management.

17
00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:09.780
Save your level.

18
00:02:17.479 --> 00:02:19.680
Things are now ready on the UE5 end.

19
00:02:20.919 --> 00:02:26.020
Back in Twinmotion. Again, make sure the provided TM2UE file is open.

20
00:02:27.189 --> 00:02:32.289
Consider the scene graph. The project has been neatly organized using containers.

21
00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:36.889
That's good news because although you could export the whole project in one sweep,

22
00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:41.389
sometimes it pays off to break it into parts for easier management.

23
00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:48.979
For example, you could hide all containers except Open Theater to export the hero building on its own.

24
00:02:54.719 --> 00:03:01.120
The export process in itself is simple enough with the Datasmith Export feature accessible in the File menu.

25
00:03:02.169 --> 00:03:08.770
For this project, you will need four distinct exports to cater separately for the hero building,

26
00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:17.050
the context building surrounding it, vegetation and other props including characters and vehicles.

27
00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:24.879
These Datasmith files have already been extracted and provided to you so you won't need to waste time on that process.

28
00:03:25.479 --> 00:03:30.979
I will mention this though, setting the time of day prior to exporting may make a difference.

29
00:03:31.479 --> 00:03:38.080
For example, animated cars at night display headlights and taillight effects that are based on decals.

30
00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:42.229
These will not export if the time of day is set in daytime.

31
00:03:45.199 --> 00:03:48.199
Next, we import these Datasmith into Unreal Engine.
